# The answer arrives as a few glanceable keywords

## Story

As a sales rep on a live call,
when the prospect asks a technical question I want a couple of big, glanceable keywords that answer it and name which of our documents they came from,
so that I can say the answer out loud without ever looking away from the customer — and I want nothing at all when the system isn't sure.

## What this is

When a technical question comes in, this piece looks through the company's
product docs, finds the passage that best answers it, and boils that answer
down to a couple of short, glanceable keywords — the kind you catch out of the
corner of your eye and speak, not sentences you have to stop and read. The
first keyword always carries the actual answer. It also always names the
document the answer came from, so the rep can trust it and, if pushed, point to
where it's written.

Confidence is everything here. A blank screen costs the rep nothing; a
confidently wrong keyword can cost them the deal. So it only shows a card when
it's genuinely confident — our bar is being at least 60% sure. Anything below
that shows nothing at all, which means out-of-scope questions and small talk
naturally land on the empty screen.

## What "done" looks like

- At most three keywords, each one short and glanceable, with the first
  carrying the answer.
- The keywords are always grounded in the passage it actually found — never
  invented. If it can't phrase good keywords from that passage, it falls back
  to the passage's own section heading rather than making something up.
- It always names the source document. It never shows keywords with no source
  attached to them.
- It only surfaces an answer when it's confident enough — at least 60% sure.
  Below that, it shows nothing. An uncertain, shaky match should land below the
  bar, not scrape just over it.
- It never crashes. Any trouble at all simply means "show nothing."
- The whole lookup stays comfortably inside the call's few-second budget, so
  the answer is never late enough to be useless.
- Every time it's asked, it leaves one short, quiet note in the running log
  with the question and how confident it ended up being.
- For rehearsal and testing offline, there's a practice mode with canned
  answers: a SOC 2 question, a data-retention question, and a self-hosting
  question each return their own fixed card, and anything else returns nothing.
